//! member: true
//!
//! std.x.crypto.sha2 — SHA-256, SHA-384 and SHA-512 (FIPS 180-4), HMAC
//! (FIPS 198-1 / RFC 2104) and HKDF (RFC 5869). Stability tier: x — the
//! D53 crypto ladder's first rung (campaign stdc-crypto, sprint sc16).
//!
//! **The nursery banner (D31).** `std.x.crypto.sha2` is not `std.crypto`:
//! its path, names and behaviour may change or vanish in one sprint; no
//! other std module may depend on it (it may depend on the facade, and
//! does: nothing — the module is self-contained on purpose); its reason
//! and graduation trigger live in `std/x/README.md`. HMAC and HKDF live
//! IN this module rather than beside it because a nursery resident may
//! not depend on another resident — the graduation move splits them.
//!
//! **Implemented from the FIPS 180-4 DOCUMENT, not ported.** Every
//! constant block and every schedule below cites its section of
//! NIST FIPS 180-4 (August 2015), fetched into the planning repo's
//! `refs/specs/fips-180-4.pdf`. The vectors (NIST CAVP byte-oriented
//! sets, RFC 4231, RFC 5869, Wycheproof v1) catch transcription errors;
//! the document is the authority.
//!
//! **The word currency is `wrapping[u64]`, for both cores.** SHA-256's
//! 32-bit words are carried in the low half of a `wrapping[u64]` and
//! masked after every add and rotation — the spelling sc16 chose when
//! the native rung refused the narrowing `int as wrapping[u32]`
//! (F-0089, CLOSED at the sc18 pin: s111 / wolf-lang#131 lowers it).
//! The mask discipline stands as written: every value entering a round
//! is 32-bit-clean, and every sum or rotation is masked before it is
//! stored. SHA-512's words are the full `wrapping[u64]` and need none.
//! Respelling the 256 core over `wrapping[u32]` is the recorded
//! follow-on, not this sprint's edit (the sc18 bump ruling: the diff
//! rewrites the storage layer, so it is a deliberate change with the
//! 1210-vector gate around it, never a bump side-effect).
//!
//! **Schedule words and round constants are stored as `int`.** A 32-bit
//! word always fits an `int`. A 64-bit word does NOT (`as int` is a
//! CHECKED conversion everywhere — a value with the top bit set traps
//! `overflow`, measured at these pins), so every 64-bit table in this
//! module is a PAIR of `List[int]` — high and low 32-bit halves — split
//! by `push_word_halves` from the verbatim FIPS literal and recombined
//! at the use site. `List[wrapping[u64]]` would spell it directly; the
//! native refusal that forced the pairing (F-0090) CLOSED at the sc18
//! pin (s111 / wolf-lang#132), and the direct spelling is the same
//! recorded follow-on as the 256 core's respell — the paired halves
//! stay until a sprint retires them deliberately.
//!
//! **Lanes, measured at the sc18 pin (wolf 21b129e)**: ALL THREE run
//! the cores now. The checked tier's F-0091 operator refusal — the
//! module's dark lane from sc16, "this operator in checked execution"
//! on every wrapping shift/bitwise op — closed with s111
//! (wolf-lang#130), and 18 sha2 rows flipped `unsupported` -> `run` at
//! the bump. What remains dark there is CAPACITY, not semantics: the
//! long-message, Monte Carlo and full-Wycheproof files exhaust the
//! checked tier's step/shadow-memory budgets ("step budget exhausted" /
//! "shadow-memory budget exhausted", measured per file at the bump), so
//! those nine rows keep `unsupported` with the mechanism named in the
//! ledger. Long-vector and Monte Carlo tests additionally record the
//! interpreter lane as `slow` (the sc16 ledger word): semantically
//! in-lane, skipped at the rig's per-test ceiling, re-measured at pin
//! bumps (is20's fix is merged upstream, unreleased — the flip waits on
//! the lupin release that carries it).
//!
//! **Byte sequences are `List[byte]` (sc35), AND THE INGESTION GUARD IS
//! GONE.** For eleven sprints the currency was `List[int]` with a
//! documented 0..255 element contract (§11's interim, F-0035) and a
//! `require_byte` that trapped `assert` on every element that broke it.
//! D72/s135's `byte` and s136's byte-typed builtins (wolf-lang#231) make
//! that contract the TYPE's, so the guard was unreachable code and it has
//! been deleted rather than left standing as decoration. What the guard
//! defended is unchanged and is now enforced one layer down: a
//! `List[byte]` cannot hold 300 or -1, so no wrapping cast can digest a
//! value the caller never meant. `to_hex` is this module's own hex
//! rendering so that its tests and doc examples import exactly one module
//! (the `std.bytes.from_str` precedent); `std.hex.encode` is the facade
//! spelling of the same operation.
//!
//! **Constant-time obligations (D53's retrofit clause).** c28's
//! compiler-enforced tier does not exist yet; until it does, every `pub`
//! fn below carries a `ct:` line stating its obligation class, so the
//! claims are auditable now and verifiable later. The honest summary:
//! the digest cores branch and index on PUBLIC quantities only (byte
//! counts, round numbers) — **and the one recorded exception is RETIRED
//! (sc35).** The 0..255 ingestion guard compared every input element,
//! which is exactly the kind of value-dependent branch c28 will flag, and
//! this header promised "it leaves with F-0035's real byte type". The
//! type arrived and it left: `update256`/`update512` now absorb their
//! elements with no comparison at all, so the digest path has NO
//! value-dependent branch left. `to_hex` is still NOT constant-time (it
//! indexes an alphabet by the byte's value) and says so. HMAC's key path
//! branches on the key's LENGTH alone, never on key bytes.
//!
//! No error rows anywhere in this module: every function is total over
//! its documented domain, and since sc35 there is exactly ONE failure
//! mode left — an out-of-range HKDF length, a caller contract violation
//! that traps `assert`. The other one (a non-byte element) is a type
//! error now, caught before the program runs.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// Byte <-> word helpers (the endianness seam, written and tested first:
// FIPS 180-4 §3.1 — big-endian words at the schedule and the output).
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The low-32-bit mask, as a total zero-argument function (a module
/// `const` costs an execution lane at these pins, F-0025/F-0026).
fn mask32() -> wrapping[u64] {
    0xffffffff
}

/// One big-endian 32-bit word from four bytes (FIPS 180-4 §3.1), carried
/// in the low half of a `wrapping[u64]`.
fn word32_be(b0: int, b1: int, b2: int, b3: int) -> wrapping[u64] {
    b0 as wrapping[u64] << 24 |
        b1 as wrapping[u64] << 16 |
        b2 as wrapping[u64] << 8 |
        b3 as wrapping[u64]
}

/// Append the low 32 bits of `w` to `out`, big-endian (FIPS 180-4 §3.1).
fn push_word32_be(mut out: List[byte], w: wrapping[u64]) {
    (mut out).push((w >> 24 & 0xff) as int as byte)
    (mut out).push((w >> 16 & 0xff) as int as byte)
    (mut out).push((w >> 8 & 0xff) as int as byte)
    (mut out).push((w & 0xff) as int as byte)
}

/// Append the eight bytes of `w` to `out`, big-endian (FIPS 180-4 §3.1).
fn push_word64_be(mut out: List[byte], w: wrapping[u64]) {
    (mut out).push((w >> 56 & 0xff) as int as byte)
    (mut out).push((w >> 48 & 0xff) as int as byte)
    (mut out).push((w >> 40 & 0xff) as int as byte)
    (mut out).push((w >> 32 & 0xff) as int as byte)
    (mut out).push((w >> 24 & 0xff) as int as byte)
    (mut out).push((w >> 16 & 0xff) as int as byte)
    (mut out).push((w >> 8 & 0xff) as int as byte)
    (mut out).push((w & 0xff) as int as byte)
}

/// Split a 64-bit word into its two 32-bit halves and push them onto the
/// parallel high/low lists — the storage shape every 64-bit table in
/// this module takes (`as int` is checked, so a full 64-bit word cannot
/// ride a `List[int]` element; the halves always can).
fn push_word_halves(mut hi: List[int], mut lo: List[int], w: wrapping[u64]) {
    (mut hi).push((w >> 32) as int)
    (mut lo).push((w & 0xffffffff) as int)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// FIPS 180-4 §4.1.2 / §4.1.3 — the six logical functions per core.
// ROTR n(x) is spelled (x >> n | x << (w - n)) with literal shift
// amounts; the 256 family masks back to 32 bits after every mix.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Ch(x, y, z) = (x AND y) XOR (NOT x AND z) — FIPS 180-4 (4.2)/(4.8).
/// NOT is XOR against the all-ones word; the 256 core's operands are
/// 32-bit-clean so the high garbage the XOR briefly creates is removed
/// by the caller's mask.
fn ch(x: wrapping[u64], y: wrapping[u64], z: wrapping[u64]) -> wrapping[u64] {
    x & y ^ (x ^ 0xffffffffffffffff) & z
}

/// Maj(x, y, z) = (x AND y) XOR (x AND z) XOR (y AND z) —
/// FIPS 180-4 (4.3)/(4.9).
fn maj(x: wrapping[u64], y: wrapping[u64], z: wrapping[u64]) -> wrapping[u64] {
    x & y ^ x & z ^ y & z
}

/// Sigma0{256}(x) = ROTR2 ^ ROTR13 ^ ROTR22 — FIPS 180-4 (4.4).
fn bsig0_256(x: wrapping[u64]) -> wrapping[u64] {
    ((x >> 2 | x << 30) ^ (x >> 13 | x << 19) ^ (x >> 22 | x << 10)) & mask32()
}

/// Sigma1{256}(x) = ROTR6 ^ ROTR11 ^ ROTR25 — FIPS 180-4 (4.5).
fn bsig1_256(x: wrapping[u64]) -> wrapping[u64] {
    ((x >> 6 | x << 26) ^ (x >> 11 | x << 21) ^ (x >> 25 | x << 7)) & mask32()
}

/// sigma0{256}(x) = ROTR7 ^ ROTR18 ^ SHR3 — FIPS 180-4 (4.6).
fn ssig0_256(x: wrapping[u64]) -> wrapping[u64] {
    ((x >> 7 | x << 25) ^ (x >> 18 | x << 14) ^ x >> 3) & mask32()
}

/// sigma1{256}(x) = ROTR17 ^ ROTR19 ^ SHR10 — FIPS 180-4 (4.7).
fn ssig1_256(x: wrapping[u64]) -> wrapping[u64] {
    ((x >> 17 | x << 15) ^ (x >> 19 | x << 13) ^ x >> 10) & mask32()
}

/// Sigma0{512}(x) = ROTR28 ^ ROTR34 ^ ROTR39 — FIPS 180-4 (4.10).
fn bsig0_512(x: wrapping[u64]) -> wrapping[u64] {
    (x >> 28 | x << 36) ^ (x >> 34 | x << 30) ^ (x >> 39 | x << 25)
}

/// Sigma1{512}(x) = ROTR14 ^ ROTR18 ^ ROTR41 — FIPS 180-4 (4.11).
fn bsig1_512(x: wrapping[u64]) -> wrapping[u64] {
    (x >> 14 | x << 50) ^ (x >> 18 | x << 46) ^ (x >> 41 | x << 23)
}

/// sigma0{512}(x) = ROTR1 ^ ROTR8 ^ SHR7 — FIPS 180-4 (4.12).
fn ssig0_512(x: wrapping[u64]) -> wrapping[u64] {
    (x >> 1 | x << 63) ^ (x >> 8 | x << 56) ^ x >> 7
}

/// sigma1{512}(x) = ROTR19 ^ ROTR61 ^ SHR6 — FIPS 180-4 (4.13).
fn ssig1_512(x: wrapping[u64]) -> wrapping[u64] {
    (x >> 19 | x << 45) ^ (x >> 61 | x << 3) ^ x >> 6
}

